WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.880
If the race reads the cytoskeleton at a tip, can you rig it?

2
00:00:07.783 --> 00:00:09.823
Start with what the winner's tip looks like.

3
00:00:10.717 --> 00:00:16.057
In 1999, Frank Bradke and Carlos Dotti looked at growth cones in cultured

4
00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:17.117
hippocampal neurons.

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.300
The future axon's growth cone had a looser, more dynamic actin network than its

6
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.300
siblings.

7
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:27.563
But is loose actin a cause of winning, or a consequence?

8
00:00:28.633 --> 00:00:34.193
Test it. Cytochalasin D is a drug that interferes with actin assembly.

9
00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:40.113
At stage two, before any neurite pulls ahead, apply a low dose to one growth cone

10
00:00:40.113 --> 00:00:41.113
only.

11
00:00:41.466 --> 00:00:43.026
What happens to that neurite?

12
00:00:43.846 --> 00:00:45.686
And what if you bathe the whole cell in the drug?

13
00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:55.213
The treated neurite was likely to become the axon.

14
00:00:56.166 --> 00:00:58.166
On screen is an idealized tally.

15
00:00:59.066 --> 00:01:01.126
By chance, one neurite in five would win.

16
00:01:01.946 --> 00:01:03.686
The drugged one wins far more often.

17
00:01:04.666 --> 00:01:08.166
And bathed in the drug, a neuron could grow several axons.

18
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:15.060
In 2008, Harald Witte, Dorothee Neukirchen and Frank Bradke did the same with

19
00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:16.060
microtubules.

20
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:20.023
Taxol is a drug that stabilizes microtubules.

21
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:24.423
A flash of light released a taxol analogue in one neurite.

22
00:01:25.143 --> 00:01:27.583
That neurite became more likely to be the axon.

23
00:01:28.516 --> 00:01:33.556
Low-dose taxol given to the whole neuron led several neurites to grow as axons.

24
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:39.620
Now block the positive loop everywhere, with PI3-kinase blocked throughout.

25
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.260
This is reported to leave no neurite becoming an axon.

26
00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.200
It does not free the others.

27
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.140
It stops all of them.

28
00:01:49.083 --> 00:01:50.983
Two results fit the race at once.

29
00:01:51.783 --> 00:01:53.163
A local boost rigs it.

30
00:01:53.903 --> 00:01:55.923
Blocking the loop everywhere leaves no winner.

31
00:01:56.966 --> 00:01:59.726
The several-axon result needs a second look.

32
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.410
If every neurite's supply is tripled in this model, one still wins.

33
00:02:05.883 --> 00:02:10.203
So a plain speed-up of this kind does not match the several-axon result.

34
00:02:11.266 --> 00:02:13.426
One possible reading is a threshold.

35
00:02:14.326 --> 00:02:15.746
The model does not test it.

36
00:02:16.616 --> 00:02:21.836
On that reading, the drug pushes each neurite past whatever level of loose actin or

37
00:02:21.836 --> 00:02:24.176
stable microtubules marks an axon.

38
00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.510
Each one crosses that line without having to win the race.

39
00:02:29.483 --> 00:02:32.703
Either way, the race reads the cytoskeleton at the tip.

40
00:02:33.923 --> 00:02:35.603
Next, the microtubules become tracks.
